    crm:P129i_is_subject_of <https://mbdiaries.itatti.harvard.edu/diary/1896-98/document/82> ;
    crm:P183i_has_representation <https://ids.lib.harvard.edu/ids/iiif/420766168/full/full/0/default.jpg> ;
    rdf:value """the evenings, as Miss Sellers can’t bear reading aloud. We can’t talk always, especially as we talk at meals and long after, and for hours at tea. I find Miss Sellers, charming as she is, extraordinarily tactless. She knows we always read, and she knows we want to go on. But I suppose she never thinks about other people enough to know that she ought either to disguise her dislike of reading or to go to her own sitting-room. She sits every night till 10.30 keeping us from doing all the things we want to do. 
Ash Wednesday March 3. 1897. Fiesole
We went to the Uffizi, and then to tea at Maud Cruttwell’s, where Mrs. """^^xsd:string ;
    mbdiaries-ontology:index 82 ;
